We'll use your 14 GB EBS volume (/dev/nvme1n1) again.

Part 1 — Prepare test data

First check that your volume is mounted:

lsblk

You should have something like:

nvme1n1   20G  disk  /data-ebs

Because in Lab 1 we expanded it from 14 → 20 GiB.

Check:

df -h /data-ebs

Now create some important-looking test data:

sudo mkdir -p /data-ebs/application

Create a file:

echo "Production application data" | sudo tee /data-ebs/application/data.txt

Create another:

echo "Database backup information" | sudo tee /data-ebs/application/backup.txt

Check:

ls -l /data-ebs/application

And:

cat /data-ebs/application/data.txt

You should get:

Production application data

Part 2 — Understand the Snapshot

Snapshot = At one point of time, you can take backup of disk.

Your current situation:

EC2
 │
 └── EBS 20 GiB
       │
       └── /data-ebs
             │
             ├── data.txt
             └── backup.txt

We'll create:

EBS Volume
     │
     ↓
 Snapshot

The snapshot is stored and managed by AWS.

Part 3 — Create the Snapshot

Go to:

AWS Console → EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes

Find your EBS volume attached as:

/dev/nvme1n1

Select it.

Then:

Actions → Create snapshot

Give it a description such as:

EBS-Linux-Lab2-Before-Backup

Click:

Create snapshot

Part 4 — Check Snapshot

Go to:

EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Snapshots

You should see your snapshot.

Initially you may see:

State: pending

Wait until it becomes:

State: completed

=> Important concept

Snapshot creation can happen while the volume is attached and being used.

Part 5 — Simulate a Problem

Run:

sudo rm -rf /data-ebs/application

Check:

ls -l /data-ebs

The application directory should be gone.

Now imagine:

"Oh no! My application data was deleted."

This is where the snapshot becomes useful.

Part 6 — Restore from Snapshot

Go to:

AWS → EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Snapshots

Select your snapshot.

Then:

Actions → Create volume from snapshot

AWS will ask for:

Volume type
Size
Availability Zone
Important: Availability Zone

The new EBS volume must be created in the same Availability Zone as your EC2 instance if you want to attach it to that EC2.

For example, if your EC2 is:

ap-south-1a

create the restored volume in:

ap-south-1a

Create the volume.

Part 7 — Attach the Restored Volume

Go to:

EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes

Find the new volume created from the snapshot.

Select:

Actions → Attach volume

Choose your EC2 instance.

AWS may give it a device name such as:

Choose your EC2 instance.

AWS may give it a device name such as:

/dev/sdf

Click:

Attach volume

Part 8 — Check from Linux

SSH into your EC2.

Run:

lsblk

Now you should see another disk.

For example:

nvme0n1   8G
nvme1n1  20G
nvme2n1  10G
nvme3n1  12G
nvme4n1  20G

The exact NVMe name can differ.

=> VERY IMPORTANT

Don't assume the restored volume is /dev/nvme4n1.

Use:

lsblk

to identify the newly attached disk.

You can also check:

sudo blkid

Part 9 — Mount the Restored Volume

Suppose your new restored volume is:

/dev/nvme4n1

First create a new mount point:

sudo mkdir /restore

Then mount:

sudo mount /dev/nvme4n1 /restore

Check:

df -h /restore

You should see:

data.txt
backup.txt

Read the file:

cat /restore/application/data.txt

Expected:

Production application data

=> Why is this useful in industry?

Imagine a Linux server:

EC2
 │
 └── EBS
      │
      └── /data
           │
           └── Application data

Before a risky operation, an administrator might create an EBS snapshot.

For example:

Before OS/application change
           ↓
      EBS Snapshot
           ↓
      Make changes
           ↓
       Something breaks
           ↓
      Restore/recover

Snapshots are also commonly used as part of backup and disaster-recovery strategies.
